    "text": "Let $A\\subset \\mathbb{R}^2$ be an infinite set for which there exists some $\\epsilon>0$ such that in any subset of $A$ of size $n$ there are always at least $\\epsilon n$ with no three on a line.\nIs it true that $A$ is the union of a finite number of sets where no three are on a line?",
